---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';

const description =
  'Parallel-safe isolation: every run gets its own git worktree + container, torn down automatically when the ADW exits.';
---
<DocsLayout
  title="Sandboxed runs"
  description={description}
  current="/docs/sandbox"
  prev={{ href: '/docs/run-semantics', label: 'Run semantics' }}
  next={{ href: '/docs/healer', label: 'Healer' }}
>
  <p>
    Each run executes in its own sandbox: a git worktree (branch
    <code class="hl">sssf/&lt;adw_id&gt;</code>) bind-mounted into a
    <code class="hl">sssf-runner</code> container. Multiple runs proceed in parallel without ever
    touching the project tree. When the ADW exits (success or fail), a monitor tears the sandbox
    down automatically (container + worktree).
  </p>

  <h2>Why sandboxes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Parallel-safe by construction.</strong> The cwd is never touched; each run works on
      its own branch and its own filesystem.</li>
    <li><strong>The branch is the deliverable.</strong> <code class="hl">sssf/&lt;adw_id&gt;</code>
      survives after teardown: you merge it or open a PR with your own tooling.</li>
    <li><strong>Agents cannot touch what is not theirs.</strong> The container + worktree boundary
      is isolation that <code class="hl">writes:</code> cannot provide.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How a run is sandboxed</h2>
  <pre><code>1. adw_id minted (or reused on restart)
2. worktree created on branch sssf/&lt;adw_id&gt;
3. sssf-runner container spawned, worktree bind-mounted in
4. ADW runs its normal stages inside the container
5. on exit (success or fail), the monitor tears down: container + worktree
6. branch sssf/&lt;adw_id&gt; remains as the deliverable</code></pre>
  <p>
    Each run keeps its own per-run db; a <strong>forward-only sync</strong> streams progress into
    the project db (<code class="hl">adws/data/sssf.db</code>) without lock fights, so the
    visualizer shows live progress while the run is still inside its container.
  </p>

  <h2>Commands</h2>
  <pre><code>sssf sandbox build              # build/refresh the sssf-runner image
sssf sandbox list               # show sandboxes (adw_id · status · branch · container · worktree)
sssf sweep [--days N]           # THE ONLY deleter: archive old runs + remove their container,
                                #   worktree and review record (and orphan containers)
# `sssf sandbox prune` is deprecated — cleanup is `sssf sweep` only.</code></pre>
  <p>
    Run worktrees live under <code class="hl">.worktrees/&lt;adw_id&gt;/</code> in the target repo
    (git-ignored); the run's container is <code class="hl">sssf-&lt;adw_id&gt;</code>. Nothing is
    deleted automatically: containers, worktrees, branches and uncommitted agent work survive
    for review and restart until <span class="cmd">sssf sweep</span> removes them.
  </p>
  <div class="note" data-impeccable-ignore="side-tab">
    <strong>Docker is required for sandboxed runs.</strong> <span class="cmd">sssf run</span>
    checks availability first; if docker is not running it suggests
    <span class="cmd">sssf sandbox build</span> or <code class="hl">--no-sandbox</code>.
  </div>

  <h2>Running without a sandbox</h2>
  <p>
    <code class="hl">--no-sandbox</code> runs the ADW in the current directory instead: the
    debugging path for iterating on a chain without container round-trips. Sandboxing is also
    gated by <code class="hl">sandbox.enabled</code> in <code class="hl">sssf.config.yaml</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Review the change — the container stays up</h2>
  <p>
    When a run finishes, its container is <strong>kept running</strong> (a supervisor runs the
    project's app after the ADW exits, then idles) so you can open the produced app and validate
    the change. Configure how to launch it in <code class="hl">sssf.config.yaml</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>sandbox:
  review:
    command: ["npm", "run", "dev", "--workspace=web"]  # argv list; cwd = the run worktree
    container_port: 3000                # the app's port inside the container
    instructions: "open the URL; sign in as Local Dev via the mock IDP"</code></pre>
  <p>
    The app's port is published on a <strong>random host port</strong> (loopback only), so
    concurrent runs never collide. In the <a href="/sssf/docs/visualizer">visualizer</a>, a
    run's trace page offers three actions: <strong>Prompt</strong> (the full ask the run replays),
    <strong>Logs</strong> (docker logs of the kept container), and <strong>Review</strong> (open
    the app — the URL, launch command and instructions).
  </p>

  <h2>Run control in the sandbox world</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Command</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code class="hl">sssf run stop &lt;adw_id&gt;</code></td><td>Stop the live run: the container is <strong>stopped but kept</strong> (logs + worktree stay), the run is marked failed, in-flight phases record the stop reason. Worktree, branch and any uncommitted work survive for a restart.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code class="hl">sssf run restart &lt;adw_id&gt;</code></td><td>Replay the session from the start on its existing <code class="hl">sssf/&lt;adw_id&gt;</code> branch — reusing everything the previous run did (committed state, surviving partial work, the per-run db). The run's own container is replaced (same name); nothing else is deleted.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Failure handling</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spawn failure</strong> (container or worktree fails to come up): the stuck
      container is stopped (kept for logs); the run reports the error cleanly.
      <span class="cmd">sssf sweep</span> removes leftovers.</li>
    <li><strong>Dead sandbox mid-run</strong> (container gone while the session is
      <code class="hl">running</code>): the <a href="/sssf/docs/healer">healer</a> finalizes the
      run as failed (stops what remains; deletes nothing).</li>
    <li><strong>Torn terminal state</strong> (monitor crashed before its final sync): the healer
      syncs the per-run db forward.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
